IML BASIC 68000
===============

A Microsoft-style BASIC for the IML 68000 machine, with 64 bit integers, 80 bit floating
point, long strings, structured statements (block IF, WHILE, REPEAT, SWITCH) and graphics.
It lives in ROM just above the 68000 monitor (basic.s, assembled to basic.bin and loaded
by m68k).


Starting and stopping
---------------------

From the monitor:

  B        cold start: a new, empty BASIC (also sets LOMEM/HIMEM back to their defaults)
  W        warm start: back into BASIC with the program and variables as they were

In BASIC, MONITOR (or BYE) goes back to the monitor, leaving everything in memory, so W
returns to it. After a Ctrl-Alt-End reset, W also gets the program back.

Ctrl-C stops a running program ("Break in 120"); CONT carries on from there.


Entering programs
-----------------

A line that starts with a number is stored in the program; one without a number is run
straight away. Typing just a line number deletes that line. Line numbers are 32 bits:
anything from 0 to 4294967294 can be used (RENUM tidies them up).

  10 PRINT "HELLO"
  20 GOTO 10
  RUN

Labels: a line can start with *name, and *name can then be used anywhere a line number
can: GOTO, GOSUB, THEN, ELSE, IF ... GOTO, ON ... GOTO/GOSUB, RESTORE, RUN, RESUME and
ON ERROR GOTO. Names are letters, digits and _, and upper and lower case are the same
(they are listed in upper case). The label must be the first thing on its line; it can
be alone or followed by a colon and statements. If two lines have the same label, the
first one counts. RENUM leaves labels alone, so they're a good way to keep jumps readable.
LABELS lists them.

The first jump to a label builds a cache of all the labels and the lines they're on (in
BASIC's own memory at LOMEM, room for 512 labels), so later jumps don't have to look
through the program. Any change to the program, and RUN or CLEAR, drops the cache and
the next label jump builds it again. A program with more than 512 labels still works:
the ones that don't fit in the cache are found by looking through the program.

  10 *Start: INPUT "Number"; N
  20 IF N < 0 THEN *Done
  30 GOSUB *Square: GOTO *Start
  40 *Square: PRINT N * N: RETURN
  50 *Done: PRINT "Bye"

Several statements can go on a line, separated by colons. Keywords and names are made
upper case. Keywords are whole words, so put spaces between keywords and names:
FOR I=1 TO 10 (not FORI=1TO10), GOTO 100 (not GOTO100). ? is short for PRINT and ' is
short for REM. A name that starts with FN followed by a letter is a user function (see
DEF FN), so ordinary variables can't start that way.


Memory, LOMEM and HIMEM
-----------------------

Everything BASIC keeps is in one block of memory from LOMEM to HIMEM (by default
$010000-$16FFFF, 1.4 MB):

  LOMEM          BASIC's own variables, buffers, the FOR/GOSUB/WHILE/REPEAT stack and
                 the label cache (16K)
  then           the program, growing up
  then           strings, growing up
  ...            free
  HIMEM          the variable table, growing down from HIMEM

Because it is all in that block, the block can be saved and loaded back with the bsave
and bload utilities (with a server running):

  bsave --address 10000h --length 160000h myprog.img     (the whole default block)
  bload --address 10000h myprog.img                      (then W in the monitor)

LOMEM address   Switch to the BASIC block at that address. If there isn't one there, a new
                empty one is made, with the current HIMEM (or $16FFFF if the current HIMEM
                is below the new block). This way several programs can sit in memory at
                once. LOMEM must be even and at least $10000.
HIMEM address   Set the top of the current block (the last byte the variables can use).
                Clears the variables.
LOMEM, HIMEM    As functions, give the current values: PRINT LOMEM; HIMEM

When keeping two programs, lower the first one's HIMEM below the second one's LOMEM so
their variables don't overlap:

  HIMEM &H0FFFFF            (first program: $10000-$FFFFF)
  LOMEM &H100000            (a new block for the second program, $100000-$16FFFF)
  LOMEM &H10000             (back to the first one)

FRE(0) gives the free memory.


Variables
---------

Names start with a letter, then letters and digits (up to 32 characters, all of them
significant). The last character says the type:

  A$     string, up to 65535 characters (one byte each)
  A      floating point, 80 bit Intel extended precision (about 19 digits, 1E-4951 to
         1E+4932), worked out by the machine's floating point unit
  A&     64 bit integer (-9223372036854775808 to 9223372036854775807)
  A%     32 bit integer

A, A$, A& and A% are four different variables. A variable that hasn't been set is 0 (or
"" for a string).

Numbers convert between the types as needed. A float stored in an integer variable is
rounded (halves away from zero); a 64 bit value stored in a 32 bit variable keeps its low
32 bits. Integer arithmetic is 64 bit and wraps around; it never overflows into a float.

Arrays: DIM A(10), B$(5,5), CUBE(9,19,29) (up to 8 dimensions). Indexes start at 0, so
A(10) has 11 elements, and an array can have up to 16,777,215 elements in all. An array
has to be made with DIM before it's used: using one that hasn't been gives "?Array not
dimensioned error" (so a misspelt array name is caught where it happens). Like other
variables, arrays are cleared by RUN, CLEAR, NEW and by changing the program, so DIM
them in the program itself. ARRAYS lists the arrays there are.

  10 DIM GRID%(9,9,9)             (a 10 x 10 x 10 cube of 32 bit integers)
  20 GRID%(1,2,3) = 42
  30 PRINT GRID%(1,2,3)


Numbers
-------

  123            an integer (up to 9223372036854775807; bigger is a float)
  1.5  2E10      floating point
  &HFE           hexadecimal, up to 64 bits (16 digits): PRINT &HFE prints 254
  0FEh  102080h  hexadecimal with an h after it (it has to start with a digit)

PRINT shows integers in full and floats with up to 18 significant digits (3.5, 0.3,
1.5E+20). Positive numbers get a space in front (where a minus sign would go) and every
number gets a space after it, as in Microsoft BASIC.


Operators
---------

From highest to lowest priority:

  ^                  power (2^10 = 1024; 2^3^2 = 64, worked out left to right)
  -                  negation (-2^2 = -4)
  * /                multiply, divide (/ always gives a float: 7/2 = 3.5)
  \                  integer divide (7\2 = 3, rounding toward zero)
  MOD                remainder (it has the sign of the left side: -7 MOD 3 = -1)
  + -                add, subtract; + also joins strings
  = <> < > <= >=     compare numbers or strings; true is -1, false is 0
  NOT                bitwise not (64 bits)
  AND
  OR
  XOR

AND, OR, XOR, NOT, \ and MOD work on integers (floats are rounded first), so they work
as both logical and bitwise operators: 5 OR 2 = 7, 2>1 AND 3>2 = -1.


Statements
----------

ARRAYS
    List the arrays, oldest first: the name with the largest index of each dimension (as
    in the DIM), the number of elements, and the bytes it takes (for a string array,
    without the strings themselves):
      ARY%(100000)            100001 elements, 400020 bytes
      CUBE(9,19,29)           6000 elements, 60024 bytes
CALL addr [, d0, d1, ...]
    Call a machine language routine at addr (in supervisor mode; return with RTS). Up to
    eight more values are put in D0-D7 first. See also the CALL() function.
CASE value [, value ...]
    See SWITCH.
CLEAR
    Forget all variables and arrays, the FOR/GOSUB/WHILE/REPEAT stack and the READ
    position.
CLS
    Clear the text screen and put the cursor at the top left.
COLOR(foreground [, background])
    Text colors, 0-15.
CONT
    Carry on after Ctrl-C or STOP.
DEF FNname[(param, ...)] = expression
    Define a function, in a program (not as a direct command). The name has a type like a
    variable (FNA, FNA%, FNA&, FNA$), and the result is converted to it. Up to 8
    parameters; they are local to the function, so they don't change variables with the
    same names. Other variables in the expression are the program's own. A function can
    use other functions. Redefining it replaces the old definition.
      10 DEF FNSQ(X) = X*X
      20 DEF FNHYP(A, B) = SQR(FNSQ(A) + FNSQ(B))
      30 DEF FNNAME$(F$, L$) = F$ + " " + L$
      40 PRINT FNHYP(3, 4); FNNAME$("Ada", "Lovelace")
DATA item, item, ...
    Values for READ: numbers, or text (in quotes if it has commas, colons or spaces at
    either end).
DEFAULT
    See SWITCH.
DELETE from-to
    Delete program lines: DELETE 100, DELETE 100-200, DELETE -50, DELETE 300-.
DIM name(size [, size ...]) [, ...]
    Make arrays.
ELSE, ELSEIF, ENDIF
    See IF.
END
    End the program.
ERROR n
    Cause error number n (1-255), as if it had happened: an ON ERROR handler sees it in
    ERR. Numbers BASIC doesn't use print as "?Unprintable n error".
ENDSWITCH
    See SWITCH.
FOR var = start TO end [STEP step] ... NEXT [var [, var ...]]
    A counted loop. The variable can be any numeric type. The loop always runs at least
    once (as on the 8 bit Microsoft BASICs). NEXT without a name ends the innermost loop.
GOSUB line ... RETURN
    Call a subroutine; RETURN goes back to just after the GOSUB.
GOTO line
    Jump to a line.
IF condition THEN statements [ELSE statements]
IF condition THEN line [ELSE line]
IF condition GOTO line [ELSE ...]
    On one line: the statements after THEN run if the condition isn't 0, otherwise the
    ones after ELSE. IFs can be nested on a line; each ELSE belongs to the nearest IF.
IF condition THEN          (nothing after THEN)
   ...
[ELSEIF condition [THEN]   (as many as needed, each on a line of its own)
   ...]
[ELSE]                     (on a line of its own)
   ...
ENDIF
    A block IF over several lines: the part after the first true condition runs (or
    ELSE's, if none is true). Blocks can be nested.
INPUT ["prompt";] var [, var ...]
INPUT ["prompt",] var [, var ...]
    Read values typed at the keyboard, separated by commas. With ; after the prompt (or
    no prompt) a "? " is shown; with a comma it isn't. If what's typed doesn't fit,
    "?Redo from start" asks again.
LABELS
    List the labels and the lines they're on, in program order. A label that's used
    again further down is marked "(duplicate: not used)".
LET var = expression
    Assignment; the word LET can be left out.
LINE INPUT ["prompt";] var$
    Read a whole line (commas and quotes included) into a string variable.
LIST [from-to]
    List the program, or part of it (like DELETE). Ctrl-C stops it.
MID$(var$, start [, n]) = string
    Replace characters in a string variable, from position start (1 = the first), n of
    them at most (or as many as the new string has). The variable's length never
    changes: S$="HELLO WORLD": MID$(S$,7)="THERE" makes "HELLO THERE".
LOCATE(column, row)
LOCATE(row)
    Move the text cursor. Columns and rows start at 0 (0-79 and 0-33 in 80 columns, 0-39
    and 0-16 in 40). With just a row, the cursor goes to column 0.
LOMEM address, HIMEM address
    See "Memory, LOMEM and HIMEM".
MONITOR, BYE
    Go back to the monitor (W comes back).
NEW
    Erase the program and the variables.
ON expr GOTO line, line, ...
ON expr GOSUB line, line, ...
    Go to the first line if expr is 1, the second if it is 2, and so on. Out of range:
    nothing happens.
ON ERROR GOTO line
    From now on, an error in the program goes to that line instead of stopping (errors
    in direct commands are never trapped). ERR is the error's number and ERL its line.
    The handler finishes with RESUME. An error inside the handler stops the program as
    usual. ON ERROR GOTO 0 turns trapping off (inside a handler, it stops with the error
    being handled). RUN, CLEAR and editing the program turn it off too.
      10 ON ERROR GOTO 1000
      20 INPUT "A number"; N: PRINT 100 / N: END
      1000 PRINT "Error"; ERR; "in line"; ERL: RESUME 20
POKE addr, value
    Store a byte (0-255) at a 24 bit address.
PRINT [items]
    Print expressions. Between items: ; prints the next item right after, , moves to the
    next 14 column zone. A ; or , at the end stops the new line. TAB(n) moves to column n
    (0 is the first), SPC(n) prints n spaces.
PRINT USING format$; item [; item ...]
    Print items laid out by a format (see "PRINT USING formats" below). Items can be
    separated by ; or , (it makes no difference here); a ; or , at the end stops the new
    line.
RANDOMIZE [n]
    Accepted for compatibility; the random numbers come from the machine and need no
    seed.
READ var [, var ...]
    Take the next values from the DATA statements.
REM text  or  ' text
    A comment.
RENUM [new [, from [, step]]]
    Renumber the program: the lines from line "from" on (default: all of them) get new
    numbers starting at new (default 10), step apart (default 10). Line numbers after
    GOTO, GOSUB, THEN, ELSE, ON ... GOTO/GOSUB, ON ERROR GOTO, RESTORE, RUN and RESUME are
    changed to match; numbers anywhere else (in strings, DATA, REM, or compared with ERL)
    are not. A reference to a line that doesn't exist is left as it is, with a warning
    ("Undefined line 9999 in 40"). RENUM refuses (Illegal function call) if the new
    numbers would run past 4294967294 or come before a line that isn't being renumbered.
    Examples: RENUM, RENUM 1000, RENUM 500,300,5, RENUM ,,1.
REPEAT ... UNTIL condition
    A loop that runs until the condition is true (always at least once).
RESTORE [line]
    READ starts again from the first DATA statement (or the first one from that line).
RESUME            (or RESUME 0)
RESUME NEXT
RESUME line
    End an ON ERROR handler: run the statement that failed again, carry on with the
    statement after it, or go to a line.
RETURN
    See GOSUB.
RUN [line]
    Clear the variables and run the program (from the start, or from that line).
STOP
    Stop the program as if Ctrl-C had been pressed (CONT carries on).
SWAP var, var
    Exchange the values of two variables (or array elements) of the same type.
SWITCH expression
CASE value [, value ...]
    ...
CASE ...
    ...
DEFAULT
    ...
ENDSWITCH
    Runs the statements after the first CASE with a value equal to the expression (number
    or string), up to the next CASE, DEFAULT or ENDSWITCH. Nothing falls through from one
    case to the next. If no CASE matches, DEFAULT's statements run (if there is a
    DEFAULT). SWITCHes can be nested.
TRON, TROFF
    Trace on and off: while it's on, each line number is printed in [brackets] as it runs.
WHILE condition ... WEND
    A loop that runs while the condition is true (maybe not at all).


Functions
---------

Numbers:

  ABS(x)        absolute value
  ACS(x)        arccosine (radians)
  ASN(x)        arcsine (radians)
  ATN(x)        arctangent (radians)
  CINT(x)       round to the nearest integer (halves away from zero)
  COS(x)        cosine (x in radians)
  EXP(x)        e to the power x
  FIX(x)        integer part (rounding toward zero): FIX(-3.7) = -3
  INT(x)        the largest integer not above x: INT(-3.2) = -4. Also takes a string:
                INT("42") = 42; a string that isn't a number gives 0 (INT("4x2") = 0)
  LOG(x), LN(x) natural logarithm
  LOG10(x)      base 10 logarithm
  PI            3.14159265358979324
  RND, RND(x)   a random number from 0 up to (not including) 1
  ROOT(x, n)    the nth root of x: ROOT(27,3) = 3, ROOT(-8,3) = -2
  SGN(x)        -1, 0 or 1
  SIN(x)        sine (x in radians)
  SQR(x)        square root
  TAN(x)        tangent (x in radians)
  x ^ y         raise to a power (any real power: 2^0.5)

Strings:

  ASC(s$)            the code of the first character
  CHR$(n)            the character with code n (0-255)
  DEC(s$)            the value of a hex string, up to 64 bits: DEC("FF") = 255. A $, 0x
                     or &H in front or an h after are allowed but not needed. Anything
                     that isn't hex gives 0
  HEX$(n)            n in hex, without leading zeros: HEX$(255) = "FF". A negative
                     number is shown as its 64 bit two's complement:
                     HEX$(-255) = "FFFFFFFFFFFFFF01"
  INKEY$             the key waiting, or "" (doesn't wait)
  INSTR([start,] s$, find$)
                     where find$ is in s$ (1 = the first character), or 0
  LCASE$(s$)         lower case
  LEFT$(s$, n)       the first n characters
  LEN(s$)            the length
  MID$(s$, start [, n])
                     n characters (or the rest) from position start (1 = the first)
  RIGHT$(s$, n)      the last n characters
  SPACE$(n)          n spaces
  STR$(n)            a number as a string, like PRINT shows it: STR$(12) = " 12"
  STRING$(n, c)      n copies of a character (c is a code or a string)
  UCASE$(s$)         upper case
  VAL(s$)            the number at the start of s$, 0 if there isn't one: VAL("3.5kg") = 3.5

Errors (for ON ERROR handlers):

  ERR                the number of the last error (see the table under Errors)
  ERL                the line it happened in

User functions:

  FNname[(args)]     a function made with DEF FN

Machine and memory:

  CALL(addr [, d0, ...])
                     call machine code (like the CALL statement) and give back D0 as a
                     32 bit integer: A% = CALL(102080h)
  FRE(x)             free memory in bytes
  HIMEM, LOMEM       the current HIMEM and LOMEM
  PEEK(addr)         the byte at a 24 bit address
  POS(x)             the column the cursor is in (0 is the first)


PRINT USING formats
-------------------

The format string is printed as it is, except for fields, which are filled in with the
items one after another. If there are more items than fields, the format starts again
from the beginning.

Numbers:

  #        a digit position. PRINT USING "###"; 7 prints "  7"
  .        the decimal point: "##.##" prints 3.14159 as " 3.14" (rounded)
  ,        before the point: commas between thousands. "#,###,###.##"
  +        at the start or the end: always show the sign (+ or -)
  -        at the end: a - after negative numbers (a space after others)
  **       at the start: fill the space in front with *  ("**###.##" -> ****12.50)
  $$       at the start: a $ just in front of the number ("$$###.##" -> "  $12.50")
  **$      both
  ^^^^     at the end: exponent form ("#.##^^^^" -> 1.23E+04); one digit before the point

The # (and $$, **) positions in front of the point include the room for a minus sign,
unless the field has a + or a trailing -. A number that doesn't fit is printed in full
after a % ("##" with 1234 prints %1234).

Strings:

  !        the first character
  \  \     as many characters as the field is wide, counting both \ (padded with spaces)
  &        the whole string

  _        prints the next character as it is (_# prints #)

  PRINT USING "Name: \       \  Total: $$#,###.##"; N$; T
  PRINT USING "##.#% "; 12.34; 56.78; 90.1


Graphics
--------

GR(mode)
    Switch to a graphics mode and clear it to black:
      0  120 x 68,  16 colors      4  120 x 68,  4096 colors
      1  240 x 136, 16 colors      5  240 x 136, 4096 colors
      2  480 x 272, 16 colors      6  480 x 272, 4096 colors
TEXT(mode)
    Back to text: 8 = 40 columns, 9 = 80 columns. Clears the screen.
GRCLS
    Clear the graphics screen to black.
GRCOLOR(c)
    The color used when a drawing command doesn't give one (at the start: $FFF, white).
PLOT(x, y [, c])
    Set one pixel.
LINE(x1, y1, x2, y2 [, c])
    A line between two points.
RECTANGLE(x1, y1, x2, y2 [, c])
FILLRECTANGLE(x1, y1, x2, y2 [, c])
    A rectangle with these opposite corners, outlined or filled.
CIRCLE(x1, y1, x2, y2 [, c])
FILLCIRCLE(x1, y1, x2, y2 [, c])
    The circle that fits in that rectangle (an ellipse if it isn't square), outlined or
    filled. It can be at most 512 pixels wide and high.

Colors: 0-15 in modes 0-2 (the standard 16 color palette); in modes 4-6 a 12 bit $RGB
value, 4 bits each of red, green and blue ($F00 is red, $0F0 green, $00F blue, $FFF
white). Coordinates start at 0,0 in the top left corner; anything off the screen is
simply not drawn. The parentheses around the arguments can be left out (PLOT 10,20).

Text doesn't show in a graphics mode. When a program ends normally the picture stays on
the screen (type TEXT(9) to get the text back); an error or Ctrl-C switches to text mode
9 by itself so that the message can be seen.

  10 GR(6)
  20 FOR R=10 TO 130 STEP 10
  30 CIRCLE(240-R, 136-R, 240+R, 136+R, R*30)
  40 NEXT
  50 A$=INKEY$: IF A$="" THEN 50
  60 TEXT(9)


Errors
------

  ERR  message
    2  ?Syntax error                  something can't be understood
    7  ?Out of memory                 no room for the program, variables or strings, or
                                      too many FOR/GOSUB/WHILE/REPEAT levels (about 150)
    8  ?Undefined line                GOTO, GOSUB, RUN, RESTORE, RESUME or ON ERROR to a
                                      line that isn't there
   13  ?Type mismatch                 a string where a number belongs or the other way round
    6  ?Overflow                      a number too big (for its type, or at all)
   11  ?Division by zero
    5  ?Illegal function call         a value out of range for a statement or function
    1  ?NEXT without FOR
    3  ?RETURN without GOSUB
    4  ?Out of DATA
    9  ?Bad subscript                 an array index out of range or the wrong number of them
   35  ?Array not dimensioned         an array used before DIM made it
   10  ?Redimensioned array           DIM of an array that already exists
   15  ?String too long               a string over 65535 characters
   17  ?Can't continue                CONT when there's nothing to continue
   16  ?Formula too complex           an expression nested too deeply (or an FN that
                                      calls itself without end)
   30  ?WEND without WHILE
   31  ?UNTIL without REPEAT
   29  ?WHILE without WEND
   32  ?IF without ENDIF
   33  ?SWITCH without ENDSWITCH
   23  ?Line too long                 a program line over 500 bytes once stored
   18  ?Undefined function            FN of a function that hasn't been defined
   20  ?RESUME without error          RESUME outside an error handler
   12  ?Illegal direct                DEF FN as a direct command
    8  ?Undefined label               a jump to a *label no line starts with
       ?Unprintable n error           ERROR n with a number BASIC doesn't use

The numbers are GW-BASIC's where it has the same error. In a program, the line number
follows the message: "?Division by zero error in 120".
